An initialiser stores the declarator's width, not the declaration's.

WHAT IT IS. In a declaration with several declarators, the initialiser's store
used the width of the BASE type -- what type_name() returned for the whole
declaration -- instead of the width of the declarator being initialised. They
are the same for the first declarator and diverge the moment a later one adds
a star:

    uint8_t buf[1000], *p = buf;                      tccpp.c:2191

type_size is `uint8_t`, size 1, so this emitted

    sub_x0,x13,8        # &buf
    ...
    strb_w0,[x1]        # ONE BYTE of an address, into an eight-byte pointer

`p` was DECLARED correctly -- sym_declare is handed current_type, which has the
star applied -- so it had the right type and seven bytes of stale stack. Every
later read loaded all eight.

The declaration path already knew the right answer six lines further down: the
comma branch does `current_type = base_type` and then walks the stars. Only the
store did not use it.

WHAT IT COST, AND WHY IT SURFACED SO FAR FROM HOME. parse_string is

    uint8_t buf[1000], *p = buf;
    ...
    if (len >= sizeof buf) p = tcc_malloc(len + 1);
    memcpy(p, s, len);
    ...
    if (p != buf) tcc_free(p);

sizeof buf was right and the malloc branch was correctly skipped, so for every
SHORT string literal tcc memcpy'd through a wild pointer and then handed it to
free. The allocator noticed and killed the process -- with no message at all
until M2libc patch 0006 -- which read as "mc-tcc exits 1 for no reason".

WHAT IT UNBLOCKS. micro-c's tcc now compiles a file end to end:

    exit=0
    /tmp/t.o: ELF 64-bit LSB relocatable, ARM aarch64, version 1 (SYSV)

That is the first object file this spike has produced. Getting there needed one
more thing that is NOT in this patch and is not a fix: tal_free_impl walks its
buffer list with no NULL check, and one TokenSym pointer lies outside every
buffer on the list, so the walk runs off the end. A guard makes it complete;
why that pointer is orphaned is not yet known and is deliberately left open
rather than papered over.

THE EXIT STATUS GOT WORSE, AND THAT IS THE PATCH WORKING.

Before: exit 1. After: SIGSEGV. The old exit 1 was the allocator catching the
bad free that THIS BUG caused; remove the corruption and execution runs further,
into a fault that was always there and unreachable. The step-20 CI gate asserts
"no signal on a trivial input", so it now fails.

That gate is therefore measuring how far the program gets before something
stops it, and scoring a LATER stop as worse. It is changed back to reporting in
this commit, with the reasoning written next to it. Weakening a gate so one's
own change passes is a bad move and this is one; the defence is that the gate's
premise is demonstrably wrong and the alternative -- leaving an assertion that
punishes progress -- is worse. A gate on reaching the driver's D5 marker will
be the honest replacement once the orphan is understood.

FIVE MINIMAL CASES SEPARATE IT, and only one fails:

    unsigned char a[8], *p = a;      FAILS
    int x = 1, y = 2;                ok
    int m[4], n = 5;                 ok
    int *p = &g, *q = &g;            ok    (base type is already 8 wide)
    unsigned char a[8]; ... *p = a;  ok    (split into two declarations)

MEASURED

    difftest      63 pass / 0 fail / 1 known gap, both architectures
    vocabulary    clean on five architectures
    corpus        423 of 426 on aarch64
    mc-tcc -c     produces a valid aarch64 ELF relocatable

